  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 335 AM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nCurrently a shortwave ridge sits over North Dakota, while the\nnext big system spins over the West Coast. At the surface the\nlow pressure system from yesterday sits just south of Winnipeg,\nCanada. Wrap around moisture can still be seen over north\ncentral North Dakota, and will slowly slide east with the low\npressure's eastern movement. \n\nWith the shortwave ridge overhead today, the weather will be \nquiet, but much cooler. Expect highs in the 30s west to 50 \nsoutheast as the low pressure pulls in the cold Canadian air. \nThis weekend still looks mostly rainy as the aforementioned low \npressure system slides over the Rocky Mountains and develops a \nsurface low pressure system. As diffluent flow sets up over the \nNorth Plains early Friday morning, rain bands will move into the\nDakotas late Friday morning. With surface highs above freezing,\neven on the backside of the low, rain will fall during the day.\nThe southern James River Valley could even hit 60 degrees on \nthe warm air side of the low. Winds will also become breezy in \nthe afternoon, mainly in the west, but will remain sub-advisory \nlevel. Friday night, with low temperatures below freezing, snow \nwill likely fall across the north. However with how quick the \nsystem will be moving, expect very little snow accumulations. \nTotal QPF amounts for the system look to be around 0.20 inches \nat most, again its a quick moving low. The majority of the QPF \nwill far in the far north central, centered on the Canadian \nborder. \n\nThe rest of the forecast looks much cooler as the cold air stays\nin the Northern Plains. Near zonal flow will create little short\nwaves off the northern Rockies early next week. Tuesday there \nis a quick chance of snow in the southern half of the state. \nOtherwise look for a cooler and dryer Thanksgiving week.",
